Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 21:23:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11348/11362] Trial 2: unprompted submission refuted; the
 force-turn closure is load-bearing

Recorded in main before the branch can be lost. With the force turn at the loop
default of 3, the 27B first submitted at turn 3 in 7 of 7 cells -- distribution
{3: 7}, 21 free turns, zero submissions. Given a third free turn, every cell spent
it on more inspection.

So the grammar's value is transport efficiency, not autonomous tool use: the same
held-out mean as the control for 2,980 decode tokens against 28,027 and 175 s
against 810. That validates the operator's decision to keep the closure with data
rather than with the design argument -- without it these cells would have inspected
to the turn cap and submitted nothing.

The negative is credible because the design leaned toward the positive: more free
turns than trial 1, a falsifier stated in advance and trivially available, a
control reproducing trial 1 to the token, and window hashes matching byte-for-byte.
48 model-free grammar verdicts with 0 disagreements, after an instrument audit that
found all 3 of its first-pass disagreements in its own checker.

Caveats kept unsoftened: trial 2's grammar arm is not token-comparable with trial
1's, and random-walk windows are not comparable with prior quality measurements.

Open: the force-turn default is 3 by inheritance, not measurement. And no
adversarial review was run under the conservation constraint, which for a negative
reversing a prior positive is a deliberate gap rather than an oversight.

